response:
To prevent the planets from being too close together, you can introduce a minimum distance between them during the generation process. Here's an updated version of your `generateRandomPlanets` function:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance;
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

In this updated function, we added a `minDistance` parameter that specifies the minimum distance between the planets. If the generated planet is too close to any existing planet, it will not be added to the list. You can adjust the value of `minDistance` to control the spacing between the planets.

For example, if you want to ensure that the planets are at least 5 units apart, you can call the function like this:

```javascript
setPlanets(generateRandomPlanets(5, 5));
```

This will generate 5 planets with a minimum distance of 5 units between them.

Here's a simple React utility to remove widows in text:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text }) => {
  const [formattedText, setFormattedText] = useState(text);

  const handleRemoveWidows = () => {
    const words = formattedText.split(' ');
    const lines = [];

    for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
      const word = words[i];
      const line = lines[lines.length - 1];

      if (line && line.trim() !== '' && line.trim().length + word.length <= 80) {
        line += ` ${word}`;
      } else {
        lines.push(word);
      }
    }

    setFormattedText(lines.join(' '));
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={handleRemoveWidows}>Remove Widows</button>
      <p>{formattedText}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

To use this component, simply pass the text you want to remove widows from as a prop:

```javascript
<RemoveWidows text="Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Sed non risus. Suspendisse lectus tortor, dignissim sed, adipiscing nec, ultricies sed, dolor. Cras mattis consectetur purus, sed tincidunt magna aliquet non." />
```

This will display the original text and a button to remove widows. Clicking the button will format the text to remove any widows and display the formatted text below.

